## Why CC-BY and not MIT

A specification's value is in being *cited and implemented*. MIT is a software license: it
grants patent and copyright rights over code and says nothing about attribution of a
document. CC-BY grants exactly the freedom an implementer needs — implement this, in any
language, commercially or not — while requiring that the specification itself be attributed
when it is used as a basis. That is the licensing shape that matches the goal.

Implementations are separately licensed: the reference implementations in
[`seam-examples`](../seam-examples) are MIT, because code wants a code license.

## Attribution format

> Meng, R. (2026). *Seam Specification* (v0.1-draft). https://github.com/islent/seam

Or, in a README: *implements [Seam Specification](https://github.com/islent/seam) v0.1.*

## Status of this file

The summary above is not the license. Before tagging v1.0, the full legal code should be
vendored into this repository so that a reader does not have to follow a link to know their
rights. Until then, the linked canonical text governs.
